WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
Marvel Studios has done it again... stoked
the ire of some fans on the internet, that

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:05.380
is.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.800
While reactions to the first trailer for the
upcoming Black Widow were largely positive,

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.620
a certain subset of fans took issue with what
they perceived as the fat-shaming of the Red

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.360
Guardian, portrayed by Stranger Things' David
Harbour.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.680
The Guardian, real name Alexei Shostkov, appears
at a couple of different moments in the trailer.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.210
Russia's answer to Captain America is portrayed
as being perhaps a decade or two past his

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:31.970
prime, and suffice to say that Harbour — never
the trimmest of actors — didn't exactly

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.220
get jacked for the role.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.430
This is obviously by design, as we first see
Shostakov flexing his admittedly considerable

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:43.970
muscles in a mirror, then attempting to squeeze
into his old suit. He does, then proudly announces

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:48.670
to his "family" — Scarlett Johansson's Natasha
Romanoff , Florence Pugh's Yelena Belova,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.540
and Rachel Weisz's Melina Vostokoff — that
it still fits.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.260
"Yeah! Still fits."

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.960
That's when Vostokoff says:

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.600
"You got fat."

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.300
However, in the spot's very next scene, we
see that the Guardian's added heft doesn't

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.760
seem to be slowing him down much, as he single-handedly
takes on the villainous Taskmaster.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:13.860
The response from some fans online was highly
reminiscent of the "Fat Thor" controversy

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.110
after the release of Avengers: Endgame. Many
were incredulous that Marvel appeared not

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.220
to have learned anything from said controversy,
including Twitter user @The_GothDaddy, who

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:22.760
wrote:

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.240
"The Black Widow trailer looks pretty good
I'd like it more if Marvel learned their lesson

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.320
with Thor and maybe considered leaving out
yet... A n o t h e r... Dig at fat people."

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.940
User @Artists_Al agreed, writing:

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.400
"So I watched the Black Widow trailer. Is
Marvel just gonna do wall to wall fatphobic

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.400
jokes in all their movies now or....? Yeah
that's gonna be a no from me."

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.760
There were a wealth of similar tweets to be
found in the trailer's immediate wake, and

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.530
while everybody is obviously entitled to their
opinion, ours is that — as with the Endgame

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.900
controversy — the approach to Harbour's
character is being wildly misinterpreted.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.380
User @MediocreJedi contributed another critical
tweet that touched on our reasoning:

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.080
"Imma watch the hell out of #BlackWidow but
did Marvel learn ANYTHING from their Endgame

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:09.980
Thor fat joke backlash? Most women I know
find David Harbour hot. So, another fat joke?

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.280
Signed, guy who can barely fit into his 21-year-old
dress uniform but can still kick ass."

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.700
While one could certainly make the argument
that Marvel didn't exactly think through the

00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:24.310
potential ramifications before committing
to their portrayal of Red Guardian, we submit

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.439
that those accusing the studio of fatphobia
would do well to take a look at the broader

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:33.730
context of the spot. Sure, the fact that Shostakov
is old and out of shape, squeezing into his

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.329
superhero costume only for his "family" to
poke good-natured fun at him, is played for

00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:43.230
a laugh. Does this make the Red Guardian a
buffoon, a punchline, like some in the Twitterverse

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:43.760
are asserting?

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:49.439
Respectfully, we don't agree that it does.
Again, in the very next scene, we see the

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:54.340
Guardian in all his overweight glory engaging
one-on-one with one of the deadliest villains

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.790
in the Marvel universe — and everything
about his appearance in this sequence, regardless

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.380
of how many extra pounds he may be packing,
screams "utter badass."

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.819
Those raking Marvel over the coals on Twitter
obviously took away something different, and

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:12.209
that's unfortunate — but we certainly didn't
see Marvel encouraging audiences to point

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:17.329
and laugh at the hapless fat guy. What we
saw was much more along the lines of, "Hey,

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:22.120
Alexei may be past his prime; he has to struggle
to get into his old suit, and his family ribs

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:26.579
him about it. He may not have the body of
Captain America, but he can damn sure throw

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.420
down like him."

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.540
To us, this is the exact opposite of fat-shaming,
and we took a similar position in defense

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:37.569
of Fat Thor — AKA "Bro Thor." Yes, it was
a shock to the system to see the God of Thunder

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:42.159
having packed on the pounds, swilling beer
and playing video games, and Chris Hemsworth

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:47.049
leaned into the comedic aspects of his first
onscreen appearance in that state. Yes, Tony

00:03:47.049 --> 00:03:50.880
Stark made fun of him; Stark made fun of everybody,
every chance he got.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.400
But Thor's extra pounds, and the profound
depression of which they were a symptom, were

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.699
not treated as a joke. In contrast, Odinson's
descent into depression in the wake of his

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:05.239
failure to stop Thanos in Avengers: Infinity
War was treated with a great deal of sensitivity,

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.280
and the entire arc was one which we would
never have expected for the character.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:14.200
With Red Guardian and Bro Thor alike, Marvel
seems to be pretty darn consistent in its

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:19.281
message, which is a timeless one: don't judge
a book by its cover. You might be old, you

00:04:19.281 --> 00:04:23.861
might be chubby, you might be depressed; you
can be any or all of these things, and you

00:04:23.861 --> 00:04:27.680
can still be a hero. That is a message anyone
should be able to get behind.

